from pathlib import Path
import hashlib,json
base=Path('research/frontier-44-special-representation-examples-28-six-step-owner-escalation-repairs/cyclotomic_center_trace')
f=Path('items/lem-universal-cyclotomic-hecke-canonical-trace-is-perfect-and-symmetric.md');s=f.read_text();assert hashlib.sha256(f.read_bytes()).hexdigest()==json.loads((base/'preimages.json').read_text())[str(f)]
s=s.replace('Symmetrizing trace and Gram determinant; bibliographic identity checked against the author\'s publication list. Full text was not retrieved during Step 3a adjudication.','Cached complete 15-page author preprint; §3, printed pp. 5–9, especially Lemma 3.7 and Proposition 3.8. Local restricted-profile computations below reconstruct the proof; the sign alpha is obtained directly from the cyclotomic polynomial.')
start=s.index('2.2 Perfectness.');end=s.index('3.1 Base change',start)
s=s[:start]+r'''2.2 Restricted-profile zero values. For $0\le j\le n$ let $U_j$ be the span of the PBW words whose dot entries above $j$ vanish, all finite permutations included. It is a right finite-Hecke module. Multiplying its dot part by any polynomial in $X_1,\dots,X_j$ stays in $U_j$: transport the first cyclotomic relation successively only through $T_1,\dots,T_{j-1}$, so each resulting relation reduces a power of $X_i$, $i\le j$, using variables of index at most $i$ and finite words of indices below $i$; repeated Bernstein straightening and the bounded reduction of [F1] therefore never introduce a variable above $j$. In particular $U_j$ is the dot algebra on its first $j$ variables times the finite Hecke algebra. For $1\le k<\ell$ and $h\in U_j$, $\tau(X_{j+1}^kh)=0$, directly from the bounded basis, since the new exponent is positive and bounded. This statement concerns $h\in U_j$, rather than arbitrary $h\in H_n$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

2.3 Transferred restricted-profile powers. Write $q=q_H$. For $1\le i\le j+1\le n$, $1\le k\le\ell$ and $h\in U_j$ we claim $\tau(X_{j+1}^kh)=q^{i-j-1}\tau(T_j\cdots T_iX_i^kT_i\cdots T_jh)$, with empty chains when $i=j+1$. The mixed relations give $X_i^k=q^{-1}T_{i-1}X_{i-1}^kT_{i-1}+q^{-1}(q-1)\sum_{c=1}^{k-1}X_i^cX_{i-1}^{k-c}T_{i-1}$, by induction on $k$ using that $T_{i-1}$ commutes with $X_{i-1}X_i$. To descend from $i$ to $i-1$, it suffices to show every correction has trace zero. Put $A=T_j\cdots T_i$, $B=T_i\cdots T_j$. For $0<c<k$, the mixed relation and the formula $X_{j+1}=q^{i-j-1}AX_iB$ give the exact identity $AX_i^cX_{i-1}^{k-c}T_{i-1}Bh=q^{j-i+1}AX_i^{c-1}X_{i-1}^{k-c-1}T_{i-1}A^{-1}X_{j+1}X_{i-1}h$: substitute $B=q^{j-i+1}X_i^{-1}A^{-1}X_{j+1}$ and use $X_iT_{i-1}X_i^{-1}=X_{i-1}^{-1}T_{i-1}X_{i-1}$. Expand $X_i^{c-1}X_{i-1}^{k-c-1}T_{i-1}A^{-1}$ in the reversed PBW order $\sum a_{w,b}T_wX^b$ using $T_a^{-1}=q^{-1}(T_a+1-q)$. Bernstein straightening swaps the two exponents or replaces them by intermediate exponents, so every exponent stays nonnegative and at most $\max(c-1,k-c-1)\le k-2$; variables have index at most $j+1$. Cyclicity from step 2.1 makes the trace of each correction a sum of traces of $X_{j+1}^{1+b_{j+1}}(X_1^{b_1}\cdots X_j^{b_j})X_{i-1}hAT_w$. The parenthesized remaining factor lies in $U_j$ by step 2.2, and $1\le1+b_{j+1}\le k-1<\ell$, so each trace is zero. Downward induction proves the claim, including $k=1$, whose correction sum is empty. [F1, F2, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]

2.4 The cyclotomic endpoint. Apply step 2.3 with $i=1$, $k=\ell$. In $\tau(q^{-j}T_j\cdots T_1X_1^\ell T_1\cdots T_jh)$ replace $X_1^\ell$ using its first cyclotomic relation. Each positive power $X_1^k$, $k<\ell$, transfers back to $X_{j+1}^k$ by step 2.3 and contributes zero by step 2.2. The constant term contributes $\alpha\tau(J_{j+1}h)$, where $\alpha=-f(0)=(-1)^{\ell+1}Q_1\cdots Q_\ell$ and $J_{j+1}=q^{-j}T_j\cdots T_1T_1\cdots T_j$. Thus $\tau(X_{j+1}^\ell h)=\alpha\tau(hJ_{j+1})$ for every $h\in U_j$. For $j=0$ this follows directly from the first polynomial; for $\ell=1$ there are no positive-power intermediate terms. The restriction on $h$ is essential. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.3, algebra]

2.5 The full Gram blocks. Let $\mathcal B_j$ and $\mathcal C_j$ be the parts of the two bases with entries above $j$ zero, and let $G_j=(\tau(bc))$ be their matrix, of size $N_j=\ell^jn!$. For new profiles $b,c$ at position $j+1$, cyclicity and commuting dots rewrite an entry as $\tau(X_{j+1}^{b+c}hJ_{j+1}^{\delta(c)})$, where $h=X_1^{b_1+c_1}\cdots X_j^{b_j+c_j}T_wT_vJ_1^{\delta(c_1)}\cdots J_j^{\delta(c_j)}\in U_j$ by step 2.2; earlier combined exponents may exceed $\ell-1$, and that same reduction argument is why this remains in $U_j$. If $b=c=0$ the block is $G_j$. If $0<b+c<\ell$ it is zero by step 2.2. If $b+c=\ell$, then $c>0$ and step 2.4 cancels the last $J_{j+1}^{-1}$, giving the block $\alpha G_j$. Order rows by $b=0,1,\dots,\ell-1$ and columns by $c=0,\ell-1,\dots,1$. The row and column at zero have only $G_j$ at their intersection. Among positive rows and columns the blocks above the diagonal have $b+c<\ell$ and vanish, the diagonal has $b+c=\ell$, and blocks below may be arbitrary. Hence $G_{j+1}$ is block lower triangular with diagonal $G_j,\alpha G_j,\dots,\alpha G_j$, so $\det G_{j+1}=\pm\alpha^{(\ell-1)N_j}(\det G_j)^\ell$. At $j=0$, $G_0=(\tau(T_wT_v))$ has entry $q^{\operatorname{length}(w)}\delta_{v,w^{-1}}$ by [F1], and its determinant is a unit. Induction gives a unit determinant for $G_n$; since $\mathcal B$ and $\mathcal C$ are bases, the pairing identifies $H_n$ with its $R$-dual. [F1, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.4, algebra]

'''+s[end:]
s=s.replace('[step 1.1, step 2.2] ∎','[step 1.1, step 2.5] ∎')
s=s.replace('The monic scalar sign $\\alpha=(-1)^{\\ell+1}Q_1\\cdots Q_\\ell$ is forced by the presentation and is a unit either way.','The scalar $\\alpha=(-1)^{\\ell+1}Q_1\\cdots Q_\\ell$ is forced by the first cyclotomic polynomial. At rank one and level one it is $Q_1$, which fixes the sign independently of a printed source formula. Exact universal PBW is a required input; unresolved prerequisites of that supplier hold this carrier and its consumers.')
f.write_text(s)
